FEX: a guest address base for 32-bit guests and a 64-bit low window (Darwin host)
===================================================================================

STATUS: applied on the guest tree, builds; static and dynamic i386 programs,
x86-64 ET_EXEC tools and the i386 Steam client run under lxrun
(benchmarks/stage7-guest-base.txt, stage8-steam-zero-vm.txt).

Why: arm64 macOS gives a user task nothing below 4 GiB -- not at link time
and not at run time (benchmarks/stage7-guest-base.txt) -- and FEX's 32-bit
mode assumes host address == guest address everywhere (MemAllocator32Bit,
compat_ptr, the JIT, the i386 signal frames, the ELF loader). With
GUESTBASE=1 FEX reserves a 4 GiB PROT_NONE window at start-up and every
32-bit guest address is host = window + guest.

Which Linux dependency it removes: the identity mapping of the low 4 GiB
(a Linux/x86 assumption, not a kernel feature). It is what makes the i386
Steam client and the non-PIE x86-64 tools of pressure-vessel loadable here.

Shape (one choke point per layer):
  JIT   Addressing.cpp: LoadEffectiveAddress adds the base when it produces
        an address to dereference; AddressModes with AddrSize == i64Bit carry
        a host Base already (MakeSegmentAddress / GuestToHost results).
        AppendSegmentOffset -> GuestToHost. Push/PushTwo/Pop keep the guest
        SP a guest value and address memory through the base. MemSet/MemCpy
        fold the base into their segment operand. The base itself is static
        register StaticRegisters[8] (x19 in 32-bit mode, mirrored in
        gregs[8], never used by a 32-bit guest), read with _LoadRegister at
        no cost. Core.cpp fetches guest code at GuestCodeBase() + RIP.
  C++   compat_ptr<T> (x32/Types.h) is the choke point for typed guest
        pointers; the raw casts in x32/FD.cpp, Socket.cpp, IoctlEmulation.cpp,
        the i386 frame builders/restorers, the ELF loader's stack and bss
        writes, the VDSO stub writer and clone's tid writes go through
        G2H(). MemAllocator32Bit maps at base + page with MAP_FIXED inside
        the window (its bitset decides NOREPLACE), returns guest addresses,
        and hands freed pages back to the reservation. HandleSegfault maps
        a host fault address back into the guest.
  Runtime (runtime/gbase.c): FEX announces the base with private syscall
        0x4C580030; the dispatcher adds it to every pointer argument below
        4 GiB of the ~120 syscalls it serves (table by aarch64 number, with
        fcntl/ioctl/futex/prctl/semctl decided by their command), which is
        what covers FEX's ~240 passthrough syscalls without touching them.

Upstreamable: as a real "guest VA base" feature, plausibly -- Windows ARM64
(WOW64) has the same low-memory constraint in a milder form. Maintenance:
~380 lines over 26 files, most of them mechanical G2H() wraps; the design
survives rebases because the choke points are stable.

Applied on FEX-2609-113-g08f451d3b together with fex-lxrt-wx.patch and
fex-lxrt-x18.patch. ORDER: x18, then guest-base, then wx. Note that
`git checkout -- .` reverts all three; re-apply all of them.

Also in this patch (found by symbolising each i386 fault, one per rebuild):
  - InitAllocator: this commit used MemAllocatorPassThrough for 32-bit
    guests (the bitset allocator was dead code); with a base it now returns
    Create32BitAllocator() and skips the jemalloc-exhaustion loop, which
    never terminates on Darwin.
  - GuestMprotect: mprotect the host page, track the guest address.
  - ELFCodeLoader: vsyscall stub memcpy/mprotect, AT_PLATFORM/AT_RANDOM/
    AT_EXECFN writes, all through G2H.
  - Frontend decoder: CheckRangeExecutable receives host code pointers but
    queries the guest-keyed executable-range tracking; the guest address is
    recovered by subtracting GuestCodeBase().
  - GuestCodeBase() ternary truncation (ROOT CAUSE of the i386 SIGSEGV
    after all prior fixes, MEASURED): `Config.Is64BitMode ? 0 : Config.GuestBase`
    silently dropped everything above bit 15 of the base -- a debug print
    showed Config.GuestBase itself holding the correct 0x7000004000 while
    the ternary's result was 0x4000. Both operands are FEXCore::Config::
    Getter<...> objects (Value<T> subclasses with an int-convertible
    literal 0 on one side and a converting constructor on Value<uint64_t>);
    the ternary's common-type deduction settles on the Value<uint64_t>
    class type instead of the built-in uint64_t, and something on that path
    loses the high bits. Fixed by reading each operand into a plain
    uint64_t/bool local first, so the ternary only ever sees built-in types.
  - VDSO symbol parser (LoadGuestVDSOSymbols): reads the guest VDSO image
    through the host view, keeps the sigreturn/callback symbols as guest
    addresses (needed once the VDSO thunk is installed, Milestone 9).

  - InitAllocator: with a base, SetupHooks is skipped as in 64-bit mode.
    Its 64-bit allocator steals every free range above 4 GiB and later
    madvise(MADV_DONTNEED)s it; libmalloc of the same host process had grown
    into one of those ranges and objc's class_rw_t for OS_xpc_pipe read back
    as zeros, so every fork child died in xpc_atfork_child (MEASURED).
  - CloneHandler: on lxrt (NoSharedVMProcesses) a new process with CLONE_VM
    (posix_spawn's CLONE_VM|CLONE_VFORK) drops CLONE_VM and goes through
    ForkGuest's fork-then-wait vfork path: the runtime cannot share an
    address space between processes, and the host clone path ran the child
    on a copy with the parent's fork locks never released (MEASURED: a
    clobbered FEX global, SIGSEGV in pthread_rwlock_rdlock).

  - x86 clone (x32 and x64): the syscall thunk bases pointer-typed
    parameters, but clone's stack (and x64's tls) become the child's RSP /
    FS base -- H2G hands back the guest value (Clone stack/tls, MEASURED:
    every posix_spawn child SIGSEGV'd, tests/elf/i386_spawn.c).
  - Shm attaches (MemAllocator32Bit::Shmat/Shmdt) are 16 KiB aligned and
    sized under a base (Darwin maps shared memory in host pages); the x32
    ipc() multiplexer's raw guest pointers go through G2H.

  - MemAllocator32Bit::Mmap: under a base, MAP_SHARED mappings start on a
    host page (16 KiB) so the runtime can map them shared instead of placing
    a private copy (MEASURED: the Steam client's ValveIPCSharedObj was a
    private copy, never shared with steamwebhelper, and the client crashed).

ALSO (64-bit low window): x86-64 ET_EXEC images (the whole Steam runtime
tooling) get guest 0 < x < 4 GiB at a power-of-two base, identity above;
flag-free clz/ubfx/add translation in the JIT, idempotent on host
addresses. See benchmarks/stage7-guest-base.txt, '64-BIT LOW WINDOW'.


  - Guest signal frames: FEX writes the frame through a host pointer, and
    the i386 rt frame's pinfo/puc were that host pointer truncated to 32
    bits (MEASURED: +0x38000 off under base 0x7000038000, so the Steam
    client's breakpad handler read garbage for siginfo and ucontext). They
    and the fault address si_addr (i386 and x86-64) now go through H2G.
  - Guest signal return: RestoreThreadState read the host-stack pointer FEX
    keeps on the guest stack through the raw guest SP (MEASURED: every i386
    handler that returned faulted inside FEX's SIGILL handler at guest
    ESP-8 as a host address, forever; the Steam client's main loop then
    stalled). Now through G2H.
  - x32 set_robust_list stored the thunk's host pointer; get_robust_list
    returned it truncated (MEASURED: head 0xf73ef9ac, futex_offset read 0
    instead of -20; the Steam client checks this and deliberately crashes,
    steamclient.so userinterface.cpp). Stored as the guest address now.
    Probe: tests/elf/i386_robust.c.
  - AVX2 gathers (VPGATHER*/VGATHER*, emulated per lane on ASIMD) computed
    each element address from the guest's base register and index without
    the guest base (MEASURED: the i386 Steam client and gldriverquery
    faulted forever at 'ld1 {v22.s}[0], [x1]' with x1 a raw guest address).
    Emulate128BitGather now adds the base (or the 64-bit window).
  - MemAllocator32Bit::Mmap searched PagesLength + 3 pages for an aligned
    MAP_SHARED mapping, which aligns the start but not the tail: a
    neighbour could sit in the rest of the last host page and the runtime
    had to fall back to a private copy (MEASURED: the client's
    u501-Shm_* IPC objects). Searches AlignUp(PagesLength, 4) + 3 now.
- Call-ret stack guards are 16 KiB (CALLRET_GUARD_SIZE): a 4 KiB guard shares its 16 KiB host page with the stack, so it was never enforced; an unbalanced call/ret stream (lazy PLT binds return with ret) walked into neighbouring memory and a matching {guest, host} pair sent a ret into garbage host code (i386 Steam client crash after ~4 min). tests/elf/i386_callret.c; measured: guard faults 0 before, 6 after.
- SMC tracking protects and unprotects whole host pages (16 KiB under the runtime, LxrtJit::Available()) for 64-bit guests: a 4 KiB write-protect only changed the union of its host page, guest self-modifying code next to a writable neighbour went unseen and stale translations ran -- V8's WebAssembly jump-table patching recompiled forever ("V8 process OOM (Exceeding maximum wasm committed code space)" in the steamwebhelper renderer). 32-bit guests stay at 4 KiB: host-page mode made the i386 client crash within minutes (~1500 SIGBUS/s of invalidations). The running-block check stays per 4 KiB page. tests/elf/i386_smc_subpage.c now documents the 32-bit gap.
- 64-bit low window also for wine* executables (and FEX_LOWWINDOW=1): Windows needs the low 4 GiB (KUSER_SHARED_DATA at 0x7ffe0000, PE images, WoW64), which Darwin never maps (MEASURED: wine 'failed to map the shared user data: c0000017').
- Under the runtime, guest memory is mapped/protected without PROT_EXEC for 64-bit guests too: W|X became Darwin MAP_JIT whose per-thread W^X switch flipped on every guest data store (wineboot fault storm).
- MemAllocator32Bit::Munmap with a guest base re-reserves the whole range with ONE PROT_NONE mapping instead of one per 4 KiB page: every per-page mapping became its own host VM object and map entry, and wine's multi-GiB reservations piled up 107k of them in 5 s (MEASURED: kernel VM.map.entries 77k -> 206k, vm.objects 95k -> 224k; left running, the "vm objects" zone reached 2.8 GB and the Mac hung in a watchdog panic). After: +~500 entries for the same run.
- MemAllocator32Bit::Munmap accepts a length that is not a page multiple (rounds it up, as Linux does) instead of EINVAL: every mapping a guest unmapped with its file size leaked; pressure-vessel's 32-bit capsule-capture-libs mapped Mesa's 154 MB driver once per DRI module and ran out of the 4 GiB (MEASURED: 3.7 GB live, NULL from malloc, SIGSEGV).
- MemAllocator32Bit::Mmap places every file mapping at a 16 KiB file offset on a host page (was: MAP_SHARED only), so the runtime maps the file instead of reading it into a private copy (MEASURED: 154 MB read per mapping of the driver).

